# Line endings, decided here rather than per machine.
#
# Windows ends a line with CRLF, Linux and macOS with LF. Without this file
# the answer comes from each developer's own `core.autocrlf`, which is a
# setting nobody remembers they have - and the two failures it causes are
# both hard to recognise:
#
#   phantom diffs   one tool writes LF, another rewrites CRLF, and git
#                   reports every line of the file as changed. A one-word
#                   edit arrives as a 500-line diff and the reviewer cannot
#                   find the actual change
#
#   broken scripts  CI runs on Linux. A shell script stored with CRLF has
#                   `#!/bin/bash\r` as its first line, so Linux looks for an
#                   interpreter literally called `bash\r` and reports `bad
#                   interpreter` - which says nothing about line endings
#
# Committed to the repository, so it applies to everyone who clones it and
# overrides whatever their local git is configured to do.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository, and check it out as LF.
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what it does with
# it. Every editor worth using on Windows reads LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Everything tracked today is text. These are here for the binaries that
# arrive with uploads in Module 3: marked binary so git never rewrites a
# byte of them, and never tries to show a diff.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
